WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.685
Client wants to send a packet to ledger, at 10.10.4.40, the computer that keeps the café's payment records.

2
00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:16.883
A packet is a small piece of data with a label in front: where it came from, and where it's going.

3
00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:24.500
On the way, it crosses two routers: machines joined to more than one network, whose job is to pass packets on.

4
00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:27.053
Nobody on the way knows the whole path.

5
00:00:27.053 --> 00:00:29.985
So how does each router know which way to send it?

6
00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:32.922
Think of a chain of parcel sorting offices.

7
00:00:32.922 --> 00:00:39.639
A sorter reads the address, drops the parcel in the van for the right direction, and never thinks about it again.

8
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:41.549
Routers work the same way.

9
00:00:41.549 --> 00:00:47.048
Each one reads the destination, hands the packet to the next machine, and forgets it.

10
00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:51.455
That hand-off is called forwarding, and the next machine is the next hop.

11
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:54.740
A sorter never asks a parcel where it's been.

12
00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.077
Nobody has the time.

13
00:00:56.077 --> 00:01:00.287
So each router decides with one thing in hand: the destination.

14
00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:03.440
It decides from a list called its routing table.

15
00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.732
Start with the simplest list that could work: one line.

16
00:01:06.732 --> 00:01:10.144
Everything goes to my gateway, the router that leads out.

17
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.912
That's the default route.

18
00:01:11.912 --> 00:01:14.936
For a machine with one way out, it's all you need.

19
00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:17.245
But router has three ways out.

20
00:01:17.245 --> 00:01:20.060
A packet for the server must leave on wan.

21
00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.675
One for ledger must go into the tunnel.

22
00:01:22.675 --> 00:01:24.284
One line can't say both.

23
00:01:24.533 --> 00:01:29.048
So a router keeps one line per block of addresses.

24
00:01:29.048 --> 00:01:32.931
A block is written like this: 10.10.4.0/24.

25
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.366
An address is 32 bits, 8 for each of its four numbers.

26
00:01:37.366 --> 00:01:42.920
The /24 says the first 24 bits, here the first three numbers, are fixed.

27
00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:49.487
So the block holds every address from 10.10.4.0 to 10.10.4.255.

28
00:01:49.487 --> 00:01:54.103
That count of fixed bits is the prefix length.

29
00:01:54.333 --> 00:02:11.940
Router's table gets a line for each of its own cards, which the kernel adds by itself, a line typed in by hand for ledger's block, and the default, now written 0.0.0.0/0: no fixed bits at all, so every address fits.

30
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.314
Watch ledger's packet arrive.

31
00:02:15.314 --> 00:02:23.584
10.10.4.40 fits 10.10.4.0/24, so router sends it into the tunnel, to router2.

32
00:02:23.833 --> 00:02:26.190
Notice it also fits the default.

33
00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:27.516
Hold that thought.

34
00:02:27.867 --> 00:02:30.394
Now look at client's table.

35
00:02:30.394 --> 00:02:37.790
The lesson gives it a broad line: every address in 10.10.0.0/16 goes to router.

36
00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:45.935
Client already had its own network, 10.10.1.0/24, straight across lan, and the default.

37
00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:50.721
A packet for peer, at 10.10.1.20, fits all three.

38
00:02:50.721 --> 00:02:52.209
Which line wins?

39
00:02:52.209 --> 00:02:53.696
Pause and guess.

40
00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.218
Most lists, we read from the top and stop at the first line that fits.

41
00:03:01.218 --> 00:03:02.760
Here, that's the default.

42
00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:09.976
So peer's packet would go to Docker's gateway, the way out to the internet, to reach a computer on client's own wire.

43
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:10.599
Well...

44
00:03:10.599 --> 00:03:14.587
that's like posting a note to your neighbour through the national hub.

45
00:03:14.967 --> 00:03:17.443
The real rule is longest prefix match.

46
00:03:17.443 --> 00:03:25.135
Of all the lines that fit, the one with the most fixed bits wins, because it describes the destination most precisely.

47
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.281
24 beats 16, which beats 0.

48
00:03:27.281 --> 00:03:32.228
So peer's packet goes straight across lan, and ledger's goes to router.

49
00:03:32.228 --> 00:03:35.084
And the order of the lines never matters.

50
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.490
A sorting office works the same way.

51
00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:44.849
The pigeonhole for one street beats the one for the whole city, which beats everything else: send it to the national hub.

52
00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:48.142
On the internet, nobody types these lines in.

53
00:03:48.142 --> 00:03:54.223
Routers tell each other which blocks they can reach, using a routing protocol called BGP.

54
00:03:54.223 --> 00:03:57.161
A full table holds around a million blocks.

55
00:03:57.433 --> 00:04:00.023
One property matters for what comes next.

56
00:04:00.023 --> 00:04:04.508
Each router decides alone, with no memory of where the packet has been.

57
00:04:04.767 --> 00:04:07.820
That lets the internet route around a broken link.

58
00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:13.376
It also means two routers whose tables disagree could pass a packet back and forth forever.

59
00:04:13.376 --> 00:04:15.880
The next section makes two of them do it.

60
00:04:16.133 --> 00:04:18.359
So how does a router know the way?

61
00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:19.079
It doesn't.

62
00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:22.809
It knows the next step: the most specific line that fits.

63
00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:24.540
One quick check.

64
00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:28.836
Client sends a packet to 1.1.1.1, out on the internet.

65
00:04:28.836 --> 00:04:31.620
Which of client's three lines wins?

66
00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:32.893
Pause and think.

67
00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:37.625
The default.

68
00:04:37.625 --> 00:04:46.823
1.1.1.1 doesn't start with 10.10, so the only line that fits is 0.0.0.0/0.

69
00:04:46.823 --> 00:04:49.807
Off to Docker's gateway.
